      "text": "We can rewrite the infinite sum as follows:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sum_{n = 0}^\\infty \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} - x^{-2^n}} &= \\sum_{n = 0}^\\infty \\frac{x^{2^n}}{x^{2 \\cdot 2^n} - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x - 1} + \\frac{x^x}{2(x^x - x^{-x})} + 1 \\\\\n&\\quad + \\frac{x^{x^x}}{3(x^{x^x} - x^{-x^x})} + \\frac{x^{x^{x^x}}}{4(x^{x^{x^x}} - x^{-x^{x^x}})} + \\dotsb.\n\\end{align*}\nIt is natural to suspect that this infinite sum has a closed form.  By taking the natural logarithm, we can find it.  We immediately see the closed form is\n\\[\\sum_{n = 0}^\\infty \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} - x^{-2^n}} = \\boxed{\\frac{\\log \\, (x^2 - 1)}{x - 1}}.\\]\nWe would rather not go to a piecewise evaluation in this situation because it would involve very complicated functions.",
