      "text": "$\\begin{aligned} \\text{Let $x = e^{2n}.$  Then}  \\\\\\\\ & \\sum_{n = 0}^\\infty \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} - x^{-2^n}}\\\\ & = \\sum_{n = 0}^\\infty \\frac{1}{e^{4n} - e^{-4n}} \\\\\n& = \\sum_{n = 0}^\\infty \\frac{1}{e^{4n(1-\\frac1e)}} \\\\\n& = \\sum_{n = 0}^\\infty  \\frac{e^{4n}}{4^n(1 - \\frac1e)^2} \\\\\n& = \\frac14 \\sum_{n = 0}^\\infty  \\frac{(e^{4n})^2}{(4^n)(1 - \\frac1e)^2}; \\text{ and (1)} \\\\\n& = \\frac14 \\sum_{n = 0}^\\infty \\frac{e^{4n}}{(1-\\frac1e)^2}. \\text{ (2) And (2) is easily\nproved by induction as follows.} \\\\\n& \\phantom{bbb} \\Upfuture\n \n\\sum_{k = 0}^{n - 1} \\frac{e^{4k}}{(1-\\frac1e)^2} =  \\frac1{(1-\\frac1e)^2} \\sum_{k = 0}^{n - 1}  e^{4k} = \\\\\n& = \\frac1{(1-\\frac1e)^2}(1 + e^{4} + e^{8} +   \\cdots  +  e^{4n} ) = \\\\\n& = \\frac1{(1-\\frac1e)^2}(1 +  e^{4}+ e^{8} +\\cdots + e^{4(n-1)})\n - \\frace{1-\\frac1e} +  \\frac e{1-\\frac1e} = \\\\\n& = \\frac{\\frac{1-\\frac1e}{1 - e^{-4}} + \\frac1{(1 - \\frac1e)^2}}{(1-\\frac1e)^2} = \\frac1{(1 - \\frac1e)^2} \\\\\n& \\qquad+ \\frac 1{(1 - \\frac1e)^2} \\frace{1-\\frac1e} = \\frac{e^{4n} + 4e^{4(n-1)}}{(1-\\frac1e)^2} \\\\\n& \\text{Thus (1) and (2) prove the result.\\text{\\hspace{12pt}} \\square} \\end{aligned}$\nWhat should I do next?\n\nWait, $ln(x + 1)$ is another integer when $x$ is odd?  \nNote that if $n$ is odd, then \\[(x^{2^{2n+1}} - x^{-2^{2n+1}})=(x^{2^{2n}} - x^{-2^{2n}})(x^{2^{2n}} + x^{-2^{2n}})=...=x^{2^{2n}}-x^{-2^{2n}}.\\]\nIn general, since $a^p-a^{-p}$ is integer for odd $p$, $a^p-a^{-p}$ is even for odd value of $p$.",
