      "text": "1. Recognize that the sum is a telescoping series.\n2. Write out the first few terms of the sum to see the pattern: \n   \\[\\sum_{n = 0}^\\infty \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} - x^{-2^n}} = 1 + \\frac{1}{x^2 - x^{-2}} + \\frac{1}{x^4 - x^{-4}} + \\frac{1}{x^8 - x^{-8}} + \\dots\\]\n   $$\n   = 1 + \\frac{1}{x^2 - x^{-2}} + \\frac{1}{x^4 - x^{-4}} + \\frac{1}{x^8 - x^{-8}} + \\dots\n   $$\n3. Each term in the sum can be written as $\\frac{1}{x^{2^n} - x^{-2^n}}$.\n4. We note that:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} - x^{-2^n}} = \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} - x^{-2^n}} = \\frac{x^{2n}}{x^{2^{n+1}} - x^{-2^{n+1}}}\n   \\]\n5. Notice that when we combine the fractions over a common denominator, the terms will cancel out:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x^{2n}}{x^{2^{n+1}} - x^{-2^{n+1}}} + \\frac{x^{2n}}{x^{2^{n+2}} - x^{-2^{n+2}}} - \\frac{x^{2n}}{x^{2^{n+3}} - x^{-2^{n+3}}}\n   \\]\n   $$\n   = \\frac{x^{2n} (x^{2^{n+2}} - x^{-2^{n+3}}) - x^{2n} (x^{2^{n+3}} - x^{-2^{n+4}})}{(x^{2^{n+1}} - x^{-2^{n+1}})(x^{2^{n+2}} - x^{-2^{n+3}})}\n   $$\n6. The numerator simplifies to $0$ because it's a telescoping sum, leaving us with:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\text{coefficient of } x^{2n} \\text{ in both denominators}}{x^{2^{n+3}} - x^{-2^{n+3}}}\n   = 0\n   \\]\n7. Thus, the entire sum becomes:\n   $$\n   0 + 0 + 0 + \\dotsb\n   $$\n8. Therefore, the given expression simplifies to $\\boxed{1}$.\n\nThis process shows the telescoping nature of the series, highlighting the simplicity of the final answer.",
